.SH "Function Documentation"
.PP 
.SS "int32_t schultz_video_create (\fBschultz_tree\fP * tree, \fBschultz_handle\fP parent, uint32_t threads, \fBschultz_handle\fP * out_node)"

.PP
Creates a video node\&. It starts empty and shows nothing\&. Give it bytes with schultz_video_write and start it with schultz_video_play\&.
.PP
The node takes the size the layout gives it and draws the picture to fit, keeping the picture's own proportions\&. It is an ordinary node: it is laid out, clipped and hit tested like any other, and a caption can be drawn over it\&.
.PP
**\fCthreads\fP says where decoding happens**, and is the same choice \fC\fBschultz_window_options\&.threads\fP\fP offers for the rasterizer\&.
.PP
.IP "\(bu" 2
\fBZero\fP decodes on whichever thread advances the tree\&. Reproducible, and enough for a small picture: a frame of 480p costs a few milliseconds and a frame budget is sixteen\&.
.IP "\(bu" 2
\fBOne or more\fP decodes ahead on that many threads of its own, created here and owned here\&. The first reads the file and drives the decoder; any beyond it are given to the decoder to split a picture between\&. A frame of 1080p costs more than a budget, so anything that size needs this\&.
.PP
.PP
A thread only ever produces pictures\&. It does not touch the tree, the draw list or any handle, so nothing else in the toolkit becomes concurrent\&.
.PP
\fBParameters\fP
.RS 4
\fItree\fP The tree to create in\&. Must not be NULL\&. 
.br
\fIparent\fP The parent node\&. Must name a live node\&. 
.br
\fIthreads\fP How many decode threads, or zero for none\&. 
.br
\fIout_node\fP Receives the new node\&. Must not be NULL\&. 
.RE
.PP
\fBReturns\fP
.RS 4
SCHULTZ_OK, SCHULTZ_ERR_INVALID_ARGUMENT, SCHULTZ_ERR_INVALID_HANDLE or SCHULTZ_ERR_OUT_OF_MEMORY\&. 
.RE
.PP

.SS "int32_t schultz_video_open_file (\fBschultz_tree\fP * tree, \fBschultz_handle\fP node, const char * path)"

.PP
Names a file for the node to read and play\&. Schultz opens the file and reads from it as it decodes, so a long film costs a buffer rather than its whole size in memory\&. The node takes over the reading; whatever was written to it before is dropped\&.
.PP
Opening does not start playing\&. Call schultz_video_play for that\&.
.PP
A node given a file can seek\&. See schultz_video_seek\&.
.PP
\fBParameters\fP
.RS 4
\fItree\fP The tree the node is in\&. Must not be NULL\&. 
.br
\fInode\fP The video node\&. 
.br
\fIpath\fP The file to play\&. Must not be NULL\&. 
.RE
.PP
\fBReturns\fP
.RS 4
SCHULTZ_OK, SCHULTZ_ERR_INVALID_ARGUMENT when path is NULL, SCHULTZ_ERR_INVALID_HANDLE when the node is not a video node, or SCHULTZ_ERR_UNREADABLE when the file cannot be opened\&. 
.RE
.PP

.SS "int32_t schultz_video_write (\fBschultz_tree\fP * tree, \fBschultz_handle\fP node, const void * bytes, uint64_t length)"

.PP
Hands the node more of the file\&. Copied, so the memory is the host's again the moment this returns\&. Bytes may arrive in any size at any rate; nothing is decoded here\&.
.PP
Writing continues to work while the node is playing, which is what a stream needs\&. A host with the whole file may write it in one call\&.
.PP
\fBEverything written is kept\fP while the node lives, because opening a WebM file means reading backwards through it\&. That is fine for a clip and wrong for an hour of television, and it is one of the things phase two fixes\&.
.PP
\fBParameters\fP
.RS 4
\fItree\fP The tree holding the node\&. Must not be NULL\&. 
.br
\fInode\fP A node from schultz_video_create\&. 
.br
\fIbytes\fP The bytes\&. Must not be NULL when length is not zero\&. 
.br
\fIlength\fP How many\&. 
.RE
.PP
\fBReturns\fP
.RS 4
SCHULTZ_OK, SCHULTZ_ERR_INVALID_ARGUMENT, SCHULTZ_ERR_INVALID_HANDLE or SCHULTZ_ERR_OUT_OF_MEMORY\&. 
.RE
.PP

.SS "uint64_t schultz_video_queued (const \fBschultz_tree\fP * tree, \fBschultz_handle\fP node)"

.PP
Returns how many bytes have been written and not yet read\&. What it is for is deciding when to write more, so a host streaming from a socket can keep a little ahead without reading the whole thing into memory\&.
.PP
\fBParameters\fP
.RS 4
\fItree\fP The tree holding the node\&. NULL yields zero\&. 
.br
\fInode\fP A node from schultz_video_create\&. 
.RE
.PP
\fBReturns\fP
.RS 4
Bytes waiting\&. 
.RE
.PP

.SS "uint64_t schultz_video_held (const \fBschultz_tree\fP * tree, \fBschultz_handle\fP node)"

.PP
How much memory a pushed stream is holding\&. The companion to schultz_video_queued, which counts what has arrived and not been read\&. This counts what is kept, which is a different and usually much smaller number: the header, and everything from the oldest picture still waiting to be decoded\&. A stream that plays for a week holds about a second of video, not a week of it\&.
.PP
A node reading a file holds nothing and answers zero\&.
.PP
\fBParameters\fP
.RS 4
\fItree\fP The tree holding the node\&. NULL yields zero\&. 
.br
\fInode\fP A node from schultz_video_create\&. 
.RE
.PP
\fBReturns\fP
.RS 4
Bytes held\&. 
.RE
.PP

.SS "int32_t schultz_video_play (\fBschultz_tree\fP * tree, \fBschultz_handle\fP node)"

.PP
Starts playing, from where it stopped\&. A node with nothing written yet starts as soon as there is enough to open\&.
.PP
\fBParameters\fP
.RS 4
\fItree\fP The tree holding the node\&. Must not be NULL\&. 
.br
\fInode\fP A node from schultz_video_create\&. 
.RE
.PP
\fBReturns\fP
.RS 4
SCHULTZ_OK, or SCHULTZ_ERR_INVALID_HANDLE\&. 
.RE
.PP

.SS "int32_t schultz_video_pause (\fBschultz_tree\fP * tree, \fBschultz_handle\fP node)"

.PP
Stops playing and holds the frame that is showing\&. 
.PP
\fBParameters\fP
.RS 4
\fItree\fP The tree holding the node\&. Must not be NULL\&. 
.br
\fInode\fP A node from schultz_video_create\&. 
.RE
.PP
\fBReturns\fP
.RS 4
SCHULTZ_OK, or SCHULTZ_ERR_INVALID_HANDLE\&. 
.RE
.PP

.SS "int32_t schultz_video_stop (\fBschultz_tree\fP * tree, \fBschultz_handle\fP node)"

.PP
Stops playing and returns to the beginning\&. The difference from schultz_video_pause is where it leaves the film\&. Pausing holds the picture where it is; stopping winds back, so the next schultz_video_play starts from the top\&.
.PP
A node that cannot seek stops where it is and plays on from there, because a stream has no beginning to return to\&.
.PP
\fBParameters\fP
.RS 4
\fItree\fP The tree the node is in\&. Must not be NULL\&. 
.br
\fInode\fP The video node\&. 
.RE
.PP
\fBReturns\fP
.RS 4
SCHULTZ_OK or SCHULTZ_ERR_INVALID_HANDLE\&. 
.RE
.PP

.SS "int32_t schultz_video_is_playing (const \fBschultz_tree\fP * tree, \fBschultz_handle\fP node)"

.PP
Reports whether it is playing\&. 
.PP
\fBParameters\fP
.RS 4
\fItree\fP The tree holding the node\&. NULL yields zero\&. 
.br
\fInode\fP A node from schultz_video_create\&. 
.RE
.PP
\fBReturns\fP
.RS 4
Nonzero while playing\&. 
.RE
.PP

.SS "int32_t schultz_video_set_looping (\fBschultz_tree\fP * tree, \fBschultz_handle\fP node, int32_t looping)"

.PP
Starts again from the beginning when the end is reached\&. Off by default, so a clip plays once and holds its last frame\&.
.PP
\fBParameters\fP
.RS 4
\fItree\fP The tree holding the node\&. Must not be NULL\&. 
.br
\fInode\fP A node from schultz_video_create\&. 
.br
\fIlooping\fP Nonzero to loop\&. 
.RE
.PP
\fBReturns\fP
.RS 4
SCHULTZ_OK, or SCHULTZ_ERR_INVALID_HANDLE\&. 
.RE
.PP

.SS "int32_t schultz_video_can_seek (const \fBschultz_tree\fP * tree, \fBschultz_handle\fP node)"

.PP
Reports whether this node can be moved to another point in the film\&. True for a file, false for a stream\&. A host building its own controls asks this to decide whether its position bar should accept a drag\&.
.PP
\fBParameters\fP
.RS 4
\fItree\fP The tree the node is in\&. 
.br
\fInode\fP The video node\&. 
.RE
.PP
\fBReturns\fP
.RS 4
Nonzero when schultz_video_seek will work\&. 
.RE
.PP

.SS "int32_t schultz_video_seek (\fBschultz_tree\fP * tree, \fBschultz_handle\fP node, uint64_t at_ms)"

.PP
Moves to a point in the film\&. \fBIt moves to the keyframe at or before the point asked for\fP, not to the point itself, because a keyframe is the last place a decoder can start\&. How far back that is depends on how the film was encoded: a second is common and several seconds happens\&. schultz_video_position afterwards says where it actually went, and is the number to show somebody rather than the one that was asked for\&.
.PP
\fBParameters\fP
.RS 4
\fItree\fP The tree the node is in\&. Must not be NULL\&. 
.br
\fInode\fP The video node\&. 
.br
\fIat_ms\fP How far into the film to move, in milliseconds\&. 
.RE
.PP
\fBReturns\fP
.RS 4
SCHULTZ_OK, SCHULTZ_ERR_INVALID_HANDLE, or SCHULTZ_ERR_UNAVAILABLE when the node holds a stream rather than a file, or nothing has been opened yet\&. 
.RE
.PP

.SS "uint64_t schultz_video_position (const \fBschultz_tree\fP * tree, \fBschultz_handle\fP node)"

.PP
Returns how far into the film the node has played, in milliseconds\&. Zero before anything is shown\&.
.PP
\fBParameters\fP
.RS 4
\fItree\fP The tree the node is in\&. 
.br
\fInode\fP The video node\&. 
.RE
.PP
\fBReturns\fP
.RS 4
The position in milliseconds\&. 
.RE
.PP

.SS "uint64_t schultz_video_duration (const \fBschultz_tree\fP * tree, \fBschultz_handle\fP node)"

.PP
Returns how long the film is, in milliseconds\&. Known once the file has been opened\&. A stream that has not said how long it is answers zero, and so does one that is still arriving\&.
.PP
\fBParameters\fP
.RS 4
\fItree\fP The tree the node is in\&. 
.br
\fInode\fP The video node\&. 
.RE
.PP
\fBReturns\fP
.RS 4
The length in milliseconds, or zero when it is not known\&. 
.RE
.PP

.SS "int32_t schultz_video_set_controls (\fBschultz_tree\fP * tree, \fBschultz_handle\fP node, uint32_t controls)"

.PP
Chooses which controls the node shows\&. The controls are child nodes, made here and destroyed here\&. Asking for a different set rebuilds the row; asking for none removes it, and the node goes back to being a picture and nothing else\&.
.PP
The position bar shows progress whatever the source is, and accepts a drag only when schultz_video_can_seek says the film can be moved about in\&.
.PP
\fBParameters\fP
.RS 4
\fItree\fP The tree the node is in\&. Must not be NULL\&. 
.br
\fInode\fP The video node\&. 
.br
\fIcontrols\fP Any set of the SCHULTZ_VIDEO_CONTROL_* flags, or SCHULTZ_VIDEO_CONTROLS_NONE\&. 
.RE
.PP
\fBReturns\fP
.RS 4
SCHULTZ_OK, SCHULTZ_ERR_INVALID_HANDLE, or SCHULTZ_ERR_OUT_OF_MEMORY\&. 
.RE
.PP

.SS "uint32_t schultz_video_controls (const \fBschultz_tree\fP * tree, \fBschultz_handle\fP node)"

.PP
Returns which controls the node shows\&. 
.PP
\fBParameters\fP
.RS 4
\fItree\fP The tree the node is in\&. 
.br
\fInode\fP The video node\&. 
.RE
.PP
\fBReturns\fP
.RS 4
The flags last set, or SCHULTZ_VIDEO_CONTROLS_NONE\&. 
.RE
.PP

.SS "int32_t schultz_video_has_sound (const \fBschultz_tree\fP * tree, \fBschultz_handle\fP node)"

.PP
Reports whether the film has a sound track this can play\&. True only once the file has been opened, it carries Opus, and the tree has a sound system\&. Everything else answers no, which is the same thing as saying the film will play silently\&.
.PP
\fBParameters\fP
.RS 4
\fItree\fP The tree the node is in\&. 
.br
\fInode\fP The video node\&. 
.RE
.PP
\fBReturns\fP
.RS 4
Nonzero when there is sound\&. 
.RE
.PP

.SS "int32_t schultz_video_set_volume (\fBschultz_tree\fP * tree, \fBschultz_handle\fP node, float volume)"

.PP
Sets how loud the film plays\&. 
.PP
\fBParameters\fP
.RS 4
\fItree\fP The tree the node is in\&. Must not be NULL\&. 
.br
\fInode\fP The video node\&. 
.br
\fIvolume\fP Zero for silence, one for as recorded\&. Clamped\&. 
.RE
.PP
\fBReturns\fP
.RS 4
SCHULTZ_OK or SCHULTZ_ERR_INVALID_HANDLE\&. 
.RE
.PP

.SS "float schultz_video_volume (const \fBschultz_tree\fP * tree, \fBschultz_handle\fP node)"

.PP
Returns how loud the film plays\&. 
.PP
\fBParameters\fP
.RS 4
\fItree\fP The tree the node is in\&. 
.br
\fInode\fP The video node\&. 
.RE
.PP
\fBReturns\fP
.RS 4
The volume last set, or one\&. 
.RE
.PP

.SS "int32_t schultz_video_set_muted (\fBschultz_tree\fP * tree, \fBschultz_handle\fP node, int32_t muted)"

.PP
Silences the film without forgetting how loud it was\&. Unmuting returns to the volume that was set, which is what a mute button has to do and what setting the volume to zero cannot\&.
.PP
\fBParameters\fP
.RS 4
\fItree\fP The tree the node is in\&. Must not be NULL\&. 
.br
\fInode\fP The video node\&. 
.br
\fImuted\fP Nonzero to silence it\&. 
.RE
.PP
\fBReturns\fP
.RS 4
SCHULTZ_OK or SCHULTZ_ERR_INVALID_HANDLE\&. 
.RE
.PP

.SS "int32_t schultz_video_is_muted (const \fBschultz_tree\fP * tree, \fBschultz_handle\fP node)"

.PP
Reports whether the film is muted\&. 
.PP
\fBParameters\fP
.RS 4
\fItree\fP The tree the node is in\&. 
.br
\fInode\fP The video node\&. 
.RE
.PP
\fBReturns\fP
.RS 4
Nonzero while muted\&. 
.RE
.PP

.SS "int32_t schultz_video_size (const \fBschultz_tree\fP * tree, \fBschultz_handle\fP node, uint32_t * out_width, uint32_t * out_height)"

.PP
Returns the size of the picture, once one is known\&. Both are zero until enough has been written to open the file, which is why this is asked rather than assumed\&.
.PP
\fBParameters\fP
.RS 4
\fItree\fP The tree holding the node\&. NULL yields an error\&. 
.br
\fInode\fP A node from schultz_video_create\&. 
.br
\fIout_width\fP Receives the width in pixels\&. Must not be NULL\&. 
.br
\fIout_height\fP Receives the height\&. Must not be NULL\&. 
.RE
.PP
\fBReturns\fP
.RS 4
SCHULTZ_OK, or SCHULTZ_ERR_INVALID_HANDLE\&. 
.RE
.PP

.SS "uint32_t schultz_video_codec (const \fBschultz_tree\fP * tree, \fBschultz_handle\fP node)"

.PP
Returns which codec the opened track carries\&. 
.PP
\fBParameters\fP
.RS 4
\fItree\fP The tree holding the node\&. NULL yields SCHULTZ_VIDEO_CODEC_NONE\&. 
.br
\fInode\fP A node from schultz_video_create\&. 
.RE
.PP
\fBReturns\fP
.RS 4
One of the SCHULTZ_VIDEO_CODEC_* values\&. 
.RE
.PP

.SS "uint64_t schultz_video_frames_shown (const \fBschultz_tree\fP * tree, \fBschultz_handle\fP node)"

.PP
Counts the frames shown since the node was created\&. Intended for tests and for a host that wants to know whether anything is actually arriving, which a still picture cannot answer on its own\&.
.PP
\fBParameters\fP
.RS 4
\fItree\fP The tree holding the node\&. NULL yields zero\&. 
.br
\fInode\fP A node from schultz_video_create\&. 
.RE
.PP
\fBReturns\fP
.RS 4
How many frames have been decoded and shown\&. 
.RE
.PP

.SS "int32_t schultz_video_encoder_create (uint32_t codec, uint32_t width, uint32_t height, uint32_t per_second, uint32_t bitrate, \fBschultz_video_encoder\fP ** out_encoder)"

.PP
Creates an encoder\&. Nothing here sends anything anywhere\&. The packets come back to the caller, which decides what they are for: a socket, a file, a recording\&. That is the whole of the arrangement, and it is deliberate -- a transport is a different problem from a codec and this toolkit solves only the second\&.
.PP
The bitrate is a target, not a limit\&. The encoder spends more on a picture that changed a lot and less on one that did not, and averages out to what was asked for over a few seconds\&.
.PP
Both are set up for real time: nothing is held back to look at the picture after it, and the encoder is asked for its fastest useful setting\&. A call cannot wait for the future, and lag is what people notice first\&.
.PP
\fBParameters\fP
.RS 4
\fIcodec\fP SCHULTZ_VIDEO_CODEC_VP8 or SCHULTZ_VIDEO_CODEC_VP9\&. 
.br
\fIwidth\fP The picture width\&. Must not be zero\&. 
.br
\fIheight\fP The picture height\&. Must not be zero\&. 
.br
\fIper_second\fP How many pictures a second are meant to arrive\&. Used for rate control, not enforced\&. Must not be zero\&. 
.br
\fIbitrate\fP The target, in bits a second\&. Must not be zero\&. 
.br
\fIout_encoder\fP Receives the encoder\&. Must not be NULL\&. 
.RE
.PP
\fBReturns\fP
.RS 4
SCHULTZ_OK, SCHULTZ_ERR_INVALID_ARGUMENT, SCHULTZ_ERR_OUT_OF_MEMORY or SCHULTZ_ERR_UNAVAILABLE when the encoder will not start\&. 
.RE
.PP

.SS "uint32_t schultz_video_encoder_codec (const \fBschultz_video_encoder\fP * encoder)"

.PP
Returns which codec an encoder is producing\&. 
.PP
\fBParameters\fP
.RS 4
\fIencoder\fP The encoder\&. NULL yields SCHULTZ_VIDEO_CODEC_NONE\&. 
.RE
.PP
\fBReturns\fP
.RS 4
One of the SCHULTZ_VIDEO_CODEC_* values\&. 
.RE
.PP

.SS "void schultz_video_encoder_destroy (\fBschultz_video_encoder\fP * encoder)"

.PP
Closes an encoder\&. Safe on NULL\&. 
.PP
\fBParameters\fP
.RS 4
\fIencoder\fP The encoder to close\&. 
.RE
.PP

.SS "int32_t schultz_video_encoder_write_frame (\fBschultz_video_encoder\fP * encoder, const uint32_t * argb, uint32_t width, uint32_t height, uint64_t when_ns)"

.PP
Hands the encoder one picture\&. Premultiplied ARGB, one word a pixel, width by height with no padding between rows -- the same shape schultz_camera_frame hands back, so a camera can be wired to an encoder without anything in between\&.
.PP
Times must go forward\&. A picture whose time is not later than the one before it is refused rather than quietly reordered, because a decoder given such a stream has no way to play it\&.
.PP
Encoding happens here, on the calling thread\&. What it produces is collected with schultz_video_encoder_read_packet, which should be called until it says there is nothing more: one picture can produce more than one packet\&.
.PP
\fBParameters\fP
.RS 4
\fIencoder\fP The encoder\&. Must not be NULL\&. 
.br
\fIargb\fP The picture\&. Must not be NULL\&. 
.br
\fIwidth\fP Its width\&. Must match what the encoder was created with\&. 
.br
\fIheight\fP Its height\&. Must match what the encoder was created with\&. 
.br
\fIwhen_ns\fP When the picture was taken, in nanoseconds\&. 
.RE
.PP
\fBReturns\fP
.RS 4
SCHULTZ_OK, SCHULTZ_ERR_INVALID_ARGUMENT when an argument is wrong or the time did not go forward, SCHULTZ_ERR_OUT_OF_MEMORY, or SCHULTZ_ERR_UNREADABLE when the encoder refused the picture\&. 
.RE
.PP

.SS "int32_t schultz_video_encoder_read_packet (\fBschultz_video_encoder\fP * encoder, const void ** out_bytes, uint64_t * out_length, uint64_t * out_when_ns, int32_t * out_keyframe)"

.PP
Takes the next packet the encoder has ready\&. Call it until it answers SCHULTZ_ERR_EXHAUSTED, which means there is nothing more from the last picture\&.
.PP
The bytes belong to the encoder and stay valid until the next call to this or to schultz_video_encoder_write_frame\&. A caller that keeps one copies it\&.
.PP
The keyframe flag is what a transport needs to know: a keyframe can be decoded on its own, and everything else depends on the packets before it\&. A receiver that lost something has to be sent one before it can show anything again\&.
.PP
\fBParameters\fP
.RS 4
\fIencoder\fP The encoder\&. Must not be NULL\&. 
.br
\fIout_bytes\fP Receives the packet\&. Must not be NULL\&. 
.br
\fIout_length\fP Receives how many bytes\&. Must not be NULL\&. 
.br
\fIout_when_ns\fP Receives the time of the picture it came from\&. May be NULL\&. 
.br
\fIout_keyframe\fP Receives nonzero when this packet is a keyframe\&. May be NULL\&. 
.RE
.PP
\fBReturns\fP
.RS 4
SCHULTZ_OK, SCHULTZ_ERR_INVALID_ARGUMENT, or SCHULTZ_ERR_EXHAUSTED when there is nothing more\&. 
.RE
.PP

.SS "int32_t schultz_video_encoder_set_bitrate (\fBschultz_video_encoder\fP * encoder, uint32_t bitrate)"

.PP
Changes the target bitrate, from the next picture onward\&. What congestion control is built on\&. A transport that sees the network getting worse turns this down and the pictures get softer; it turns it up again when there is room\&. Nothing here decides that: this is the handle, and the decision belongs to whoever is watching the network\&.
.PP
\fBParameters\fP
.RS 4
\fIencoder\fP The encoder\&. Must not be NULL\&. 
.br
\fIbitrate\fP The new target, in bits a second\&. Must not be zero\&. 
.RE
.PP
\fBReturns\fP
.RS 4
SCHULTZ_OK, SCHULTZ_ERR_INVALID_ARGUMENT, or SCHULTZ_ERR_UNAVAILABLE when the encoder refused it\&. 
.RE
.PP

.SS "int32_t schultz_video_encoder_force_keyframe (\fBschultz_video_encoder\fP * encoder)"

.PP
Makes the next picture a keyframe\&. What loss recovery is built on\&. A receiver that has lost packets cannot show anything until a keyframe reaches it, and asking for one is the only way to shorten that wait\&.
.PP
It applies to the next picture written, once\&. Asking twice before writing one is the same as asking once\&.
.PP
\fBParameters\fP
.RS 4
\fIencoder\fP The encoder\&. Must not be NULL\&. 
.RE
.PP
\fBReturns\fP
.RS 4
SCHULTZ_OK or SCHULTZ_ERR_INVALID_ARGUMENT\&. 
.RE
.PP

.SS "uint32_t schultz_video_encoder_bitrate (const \fBschultz_video_encoder\fP * encoder)"

.PP
Returns the target bitrate the encoder is working to\&. 
.PP
\fBParameters\fP
.RS 4
\fIencoder\fP The encoder\&. NULL yields zero\&. 
.RE
.PP
\fBReturns\fP
.RS 4
The target, in bits a second\&. 
.RE
.PP

.SS "int32_t schultz_video_encoder_set_speed (\fBschultz_video_encoder\fP * encoder, uint32_t speed)"

.PP
Sets how quickly the encoder works, against how good it looks\&. Zero is the slowest and the best picture; SCHULTZ_VIDEO_SPEED_MOST is the quickest and the roughest\&. The default is eight, near the quick end, because this is built for calls and lag is what people notice first\&.
.PP
\fBIt matters far more to VP9 than to VP8\&.\fP Measured on a build with no SIMD, at 320x240: VP8 takes about six milliseconds a picture whatever it is asked for, and VP9 ranges from 58 milliseconds at zero to under four at eight and above\&. Turning this down is how a host trades the processor it has for a better picture, and on VP9 there is a great deal to trade\&.
.PP
Can be changed at any time; it applies from the next picture\&.
.PP
\fBParameters\fP
.RS 4
\fIencoder\fP The encoder\&. Must not be NULL\&. 
.br
\fIspeed\fP Zero to SCHULTZ_VIDEO_SPEED_MOST\&. Above that is taken as the most\&. 
.RE
.PP
\fBReturns\fP
.RS 4
SCHULTZ_OK, SCHULTZ_ERR_INVALID_ARGUMENT, or SCHULTZ_ERR_UNAVAILABLE when the encoder refused it\&. 
.RE
.PP

.SS "uint32_t schultz_video_encoder_speed (const \fBschultz_video_encoder\fP * encoder)"

.PP
Returns how quickly the encoder has been asked to work\&. 
.PP
\fBParameters\fP
.RS 4
\fIencoder\fP The encoder\&. NULL yields zero\&. 
.RE
.PP
\fBReturns\fP
.RS 4
Zero to SCHULTZ_VIDEO_SPEED_MOST\&. 
.RE
.PP

.SS "int32_t schultz_video_decoder_create (uint32_t codec, \fBschultz_video_decoder\fP ** out_decoder)"

.PP
Creates a decoder for bare packets\&. The codec has to be said, because a bare packet does not carry it\&. A file would: that is part of what a container is for, and why the video node never has to be told\&. A receiver learns it from whatever agreed the call\&.
.PP
\fBParameters\fP
.RS 4
\fIcodec\fP SCHULTZ_VIDEO_CODEC_VP8 or SCHULTZ_VIDEO_CODEC_VP9\&. 
.br
\fIout_decoder\fP Receives the decoder\&. Must not be NULL\&. 
.RE
.PP
\fBReturns\fP
.RS 4
SCHULTZ_OK, SCHULTZ_ERR_INVALID_ARGUMENT, SCHULTZ_ERR_OUT_OF_MEMORY or SCHULTZ_ERR_UNAVAILABLE\&. 
.RE
.PP

.SS "void schultz_video_decoder_destroy (\fBschultz_video_decoder\fP * decoder)"

.PP
Closes a decoder\&. Safe on NULL\&. 
.PP
\fBParameters\fP
.RS 4
\fIdecoder\fP The decoder to close\&. 
.RE
.PP

.SS "int32_t schultz_video_decoder_write_packet (\fBschultz_video_decoder\fP * decoder, const void * bytes, uint64_t length)"

.PP
Hands the decoder one packet\&. Packets must arrive in the order they were produced\&. A decoder given the first packet after a loss shows nothing until a keyframe reaches it, which is what schultz_video_encoder_force_keyframe is for\&.
.PP
\fBParameters\fP
.RS 4
\fIdecoder\fP The decoder\&. Must not be NULL\&. 
.br
\fIbytes\fP The packet\&. Must not be NULL\&. 
.br
\fIlength\fP How many bytes\&. Must not be zero\&. 
.RE
.PP
\fBReturns\fP
.RS 4
SCHULTZ_OK, SCHULTZ_ERR_INVALID_ARGUMENT, or SCHULTZ_ERR_UNREADABLE when the packet could not be decoded\&. 
.RE
.PP

.SS "int32_t schultz_video_decoder_read_frame (\fBschultz_video_decoder\fP * decoder, const uint32_t ** out_pixels, uint32_t * out_width, uint32_t * out_height)"

.PP
Takes the next picture the decoder has ready\&. Premultiplied ARGB, one word a pixel, width by height with no padding between rows -- the same shape everything else here hands over\&.
.PP
The pixels belong to the decoder and stay valid until the next call to this or to schultz_video_decoder_write_packet\&.
.PP
\fBParameters\fP
.RS 4
\fIdecoder\fP The decoder\&. Must not be NULL\&. 
.br
\fIout_pixels\fP Receives the picture\&. Must not be NULL\&. 
.br
\fIout_width\fP Receives the width\&. May be NULL\&. 
.br
\fIout_height\fP Receives the height\&. May be NULL\&. 
.RE
.PP
\fBReturns\fP
.RS 4
SCHULTZ_OK, SCHULTZ_ERR_INVALID_ARGUMENT, SCHULTZ_ERR_EXHAUSTED when there is nothing more, or SCHULTZ_ERR_OUT_OF_MEMORY\&. 
.RE
.PP

.SS "int32_t schultz_video_writer_create (const char * path, uint32_t codec, uint32_t width, uint32_t height, \fBschultz_video_writer\fP ** out_writer)"

.PP
Starts a WebM file\&. Nothing is written until the first packet, so a sound track can still be added after this and before then\&.
.PP
\fBParameters\fP
.RS 4
\fIpath\fP The file to write\&. Overwritten if it exists\&. Must not be NULL\&. 
.br
\fIcodec\fP SCHULTZ_VIDEO_CODEC_VP8 or SCHULTZ_VIDEO_CODEC_VP9\&. It must match what the encoder is producing: a container that names the wrong codec plays as nothing\&. 
.br
\fIwidth\fP The picture width\&. Must not be zero\&. 
.br
\fIheight\fP The picture height\&. Must not be zero\&. 
.br
\fIout_writer\fP Receives the writer\&. Must not be NULL\&. 
.RE
.PP
\fBReturns\fP
.RS 4
SCHULTZ_OK, SCHULTZ_ERR_INVALID_ARGUMENT, SCHULTZ_ERR_OUT_OF_MEMORY or SCHULTZ_ERR_UNREADABLE when the file will not open\&. 
.RE
.PP

.SS "int32_t schultz_video_writer_add_sound (\fBschultz_video_writer\fP * writer, uint32_t channels)"

.PP
Adds an Opus sound track\&. Only before the first packet is written, because the tracks are described at the top of the file and nothing can be added to that list afterwards\&.
.PP
Forty eight thousand samples a second, which is what Opus works in and what schultz_audio_encoder_create produces\&.
.PP
\fBParameters\fP
.RS 4
\fIwriter\fP The writer\&. Must not be NULL\&. 
.br
\fIchannels\fP One for mono, two for stereo\&. 
.RE
.PP
\fBReturns\fP
.RS 4
SCHULTZ_OK, SCHULTZ_ERR_INVALID_ARGUMENT, or SCHULTZ_ERR_UNAVAILABLE when something has already been written\&. 
.RE
.PP

.SS "int32_t schultz_video_writer_write_picture (\fBschultz_video_writer\fP * writer, const void * bytes, uint64_t length, uint64_t when_ns, int32_t keyframe)"

.PP
Writes one picture\&. Straight from schultz_video_encoder_read_packet: the bytes, the time it carries, and whether it is a keyframe\&.
.PP
The keyframe flag is not decoration\&. It is what a player jumps to when somebody drags a slider, and this records where each one landed so that seeking the finished file works\&. A file written with every packet marked ordinary plays from the start and cannot be moved about in\&.
.PP
Times must go forward\&.
.PP
\fBParameters\fP
.RS 4
\fIwriter\fP The writer\&. Must not be NULL\&. 
.br
\fIbytes\fP The packet\&. Must not be NULL\&. 
.br
\fIlength\fP How many bytes\&. Must not be zero\&. 
.br
\fIwhen_ns\fP When it belongs, in nanoseconds from the start\&. 
.br
\fIkeyframe\fP Nonzero when the packet can be decoded on its own\&. 
.RE
.PP
\fBReturns\fP
.RS 4
SCHULTZ_OK, SCHULTZ_ERR_INVALID_ARGUMENT, SCHULTZ_ERR_OUT_OF_MEMORY or SCHULTZ_ERR_UNREADABLE when the file would not take it\&. 
.RE
.PP

.SS "int32_t schultz_video_writer_write_sound (\fBschultz_video_writer\fP * writer, const void * bytes, uint64_t length, uint64_t when_ns)"

.PP
Writes one packet of sound\&. Straight from schultz_audio_encoder_read_packet\&. Needs a sound track, added with schultz_video_writer_add_sound before anything was written\&.
.PP
Every Opus packet stands on its own, so there is no keyframe to say\&.
.PP
\fBParameters\fP
.RS 4
\fIwriter\fP The writer\&. Must not be NULL\&. 
.br
\fIbytes\fP The packet\&. Must not be NULL\&. 
.br
\fIlength\fP How many bytes\&. Must not be zero\&. 
.br
\fIwhen_ns\fP When it belongs, in nanoseconds from the start\&. 
.RE
.PP
\fBReturns\fP
.RS 4
SCHULTZ_OK, SCHULTZ_ERR_INVALID_ARGUMENT, SCHULTZ_ERR_OUT_OF_MEMORY or SCHULTZ_ERR_UNAVAILABLE when there is no sound track\&. 
.RE
.PP

.SS "int32_t schultz_video_writer_close (\fBschultz_video_writer\fP * writer)"

.PP
Finishes the file and closes it\&. This is where the parts that could only be known at the end are written: how long the film is, and the index of where each keyframe landed\&. A writer that is dropped without this leaves a file that plays but cannot be seeked in and does not know its own length\&.
.PP
The writer is released either way, so there is nothing to free afterwards and calling it twice is not possible\&. Safe on NULL\&.
.PP
\fBParameters\fP
.RS 4
\fIwriter\fP The writer to finish\&. 
.RE
.PP
\fBReturns\fP
.RS 4
SCHULTZ_OK, or SCHULTZ_ERR_UNREADABLE when the file would not take the last of it\&. 
.RE
.PP

